---
title: Layout, Axes, and Coordinates
description: Learn how responsive ranges, automatic margins, guides, band alignment, and scene coordinates work.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts gives as much space as possible to the plot while keeping chart-owned guides inside the surface. The normal path is container-responsive and uses automatic margins."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"surface-margin-and-plot-rectangle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Surface, margin, and plot rectangle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every scene has three nested regions:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"surface: scene.width × scene.height\n└─ automatic or explicit margins\n   └─ plot rectangle: scene.chart","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.chart"},{"type":"text","value":" contains:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartBounds {\n  x: number\n  y: number\n  width: number\n  height: number\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks, grids, clipping, pointer focus, and copied scale ranges use this resolved plot rectangle."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"container-responsiveness","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Container responsiveness"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Omit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":" on the DOM host or framework adapter:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<Chart\n  definition={chart}\n  height={360}\n  initialWidth={640}\n  ariaLabel=\"Monthly revenue\"\n/>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The host observes its container and coalesces width changes into an animation frame. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialWidth"},{"type":"text","value":" is used when a real width is not yet available and for deterministic server output."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aspectRatio"},{"type":"text","value":" when height should follow width:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<Chart\n  definition={chart}\n  aspectRatio={16 / 9}\n  initialWidth={640}\n  ariaLabel=\"Monthly revenue\"\n/>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set a fixed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":" only for an intentionally fixed graphic such as export, print, or email."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive definitions receive the current "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"height"},{"type":"text","value":", so presentation can adapt to the chart container:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const chart = defineChart(({ width }) => ({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: xScale,\n      axis: {\n        ticks: { count: width < 420 ? 4 : 8 },\n        tickLabels: { rotate: width < 520 ? -30 : undefined },\n      },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: yScale,\n      axis: { label: width < 480 ? undefined : 'Weekly downloads' },\n    },\n  },\n}))","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"automatic-margins","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic margins"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leave "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"margin"},{"type":"text","value":" undefined for normal charts. The layout solver accounts for:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Formatted tick-label width and height"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rotated tick-label bounds"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First and last tick overhang"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis titles and their offsets"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text-mark bounds, including anchors, pixel offsets, and rotation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Current container font metrics"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional color legends"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The solver may resolve scales and text-mark positions more than once, but marks\nrender once against the final plot rectangle. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clip: true"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the plot\nboundary authoritative, so clipped text does not expand automatic margins."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit margins lock only the sides you provide:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  margin: { left: 80 },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here the left margin is exactly "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"80"},{"type":"text","value":"; top, right, and bottom remain automatic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"margin: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" locks every side to zero:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const sparkline = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(values)],\n  guides: false,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  margin: 0,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use that combination for sparklines and intentionally chrome-free embedded graphics."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"text-measurement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text measurement"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static scenes use deterministic text estimates. The DOM host and browser\nframework adapters measure painted glyph bounds with the chart container's\ninherited font and relayout after web fonts load."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced renderers can supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"measureText"},{"type":"text","value":". Its metrics include painted x and y offsets relative to the requested anchor and baseline, not only width and height. This is necessary for correct containment of rotated and anchored labels."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic margins contain chart-owned guides and Cartesian "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":" marks. Axis\ntick labels are thinned against their measured, optionally rotated bounds.\nExplicit text placement remains responsible for data-label collisions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"axis-guide-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis guide options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each axis combines a required scale factory or instance with optional guide controls:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const x = {\n  scale: xScale,\n  grid: false,\n  axis: {\n    ticks: {\n      count: 6,\n      format: (date: Date) => monthFormatter.format(date),\n    },\n    tickLabels: { rotate: -30 },\n    label: {\n      text: 'Month',\n      offset: 12,\n      fontSize: 13,\n      fontWeight: 600,\n      fill: '#334155',\n      opacity: 0.9,\n    },\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Purpose"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure the axis or hide it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.line"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show, hide, or style the baseline"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.ticks"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure candidates, stubs, padding, and formatting"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.tickLabels"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure label rotation and collision thinning"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.label"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configure axis title text, typography, paint, and offset"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grid"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Draw or style grid lines at semantic candidates"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reverse"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reverse the responsive range"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both grids default to hidden when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grid"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted. A style object enables\nthe grid or baseline and accepts renderer-neutral stroke, opacity, width,\ndash, and line-cap fields."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Candidate generation and label layout are separate. Choose at most one of\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.ticks.count"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.ticks.spacing"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.ticks.values"},{"type":"text","value":". Grid lines\nand tick stubs use the generated candidates; label thinning does not remove\neither. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.ticks.size: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" removes stubs while retaining labels and grid\nlines."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rotation and thinning are independent. Thinning is enabled by default and\nuses measured rotated bounds:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const x = {\n  scale: xScale,\n  axis: {\n    ticks: { spacing: 80 },\n    tickLabels: {\n      rotate: -35,\n      thin: { minGap: 8, priority: 'ends', keep: importantDates },\n    },\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hard-kept labels are retained even when they collide. Values absent from the\ncandidate set add labels only."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hide one guide without removing its scale:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const x = {\n  scale: xScale,\n  axis: false,\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hide every axis and grid while keeping scales for marks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  guides: false,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set a reserved scale to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" only when no mark uses that dimension. For\nexample, a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ruleY"},{"type":"text","value":"-only chart uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales: { x: null, y: { scale: yScale } }"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"multiple-axes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multiple axes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a named scale when one coordinate system needs an independent mapping.\nDeclare whether it maps x or y, choose its axis side, then bind the relevant\nmark to its ID:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(revenue, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    lineY(margin, {\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'percent',\n      yScale: 'margin',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: dateScale },\n    y: { scale: revenueScale, axis: { label: 'Revenue' } },\n    margin: {\n      channel: 'y',\n      scale: marginScale,\n      side: 'right',\n      axis: { label: 'Margin' },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xScale"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yScale"},{"type":"text","value":" bind marks to scale IDs, not axis IDs. Axes visualize the\nscale registry entries. Multiple axes on one side stack outward and take part\nin automatic margin measurement."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scale-ranges-and-coordinate-direction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scale ranges and coordinate direction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scale factories derive domains from marks. Configured instances retain fixed\nsemantic domains. TanStack Charts supplies ranges from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.chart"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a normal cartesian chart:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"x increases from the left edge to the right edge."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"continuous y increases from the bottom edge to the top edge."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a y band scale lays categories from top to bottom."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reverse: true"},{"type":"text","value":" flips the range. It does not reorder or mutate the source domain."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for scale selection, responsive ownership, and\npixel-to-value inversion."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"continuous-viewports","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Continuous viewports"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A continuous axis can present one semantic window of a larger content domain:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const x = {\n  scale: scaleUtc().domain([historyStart, historyEnd]),\n  viewport: {\n    domain: [visibleStart, visibleEnd],\n    translate: dragOffset,\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The configured or inferred scale domain describes the complete content.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewport.domain"},{"type":"text","value":" is the committed semantic window used for mapping, axes, and\ngrid lines. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewport.translate"},{"type":"text","value":" is a transient output-space offset applied\nafter that mapping."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Viewport ownership is resolved for each mark and each axis. A mark that\nmaterializes an active viewport axis is content on that axis by default. The\ncompiler gives each such mark its own plot-bounded clip layer and applies only\nthe translations for axes it owns. Axes and grid lines stay fixed. Marks that\ndo not depend on the translated axis also stay fixed, such as a frame or a\ny-only annotation during an x drag. Custom marks can override either axis as\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'content'"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'fixed'"},{"type":"text","value":" through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InitializedMark.viewport"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks, focus layers, interaction points, and tooltip anchors use the same\npresented coordinates. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.points"},{"type":"text","value":" retains every content point, including\noff-window points, for rendering and diagnostics. The interaction host limits\npointer strategies and keyboard navigation to clipped content points whose\npresented anchors are inside the plot clip. Points from marks with fixed\nviewport ownership remain candidates outside the plot.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewportInteractionPoints(scene)"},{"type":"text","value":" returns that subset without changing\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.points"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Translation is expressed in screen-direction scene pixels: positive x moves\ncontent right, negative x moves it left, positive y moves it down, and negative\ny moves it up. Domain order and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reverse"},{"type":"text","value":" do not change those directions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This makes paged history one chart and one continuous line rather than a guide\nchart overlaid with several plot charts. During a drag, keep the committed\ndomain fixed and update only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"translate"},{"type":"text","value":". To settle one page, animate the\ntranslation to one plot width, then update the semantic domain and reset the\ntranslation to zero in the same application commit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Viewport domains accept two distinct finite numbers or two distinct finite\nDates. The scale must be configured or inferable, continuous, invertible,\nunclamped, and independently accept domain and range assignment. Band,\nordinal, quantize, clamped, and getter-only scales are rejected. An authored\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axis.viewport"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot be applied to an opaque custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScale"},{"type":"text","value":"; a custom\nresolver can instead return a complete "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ResolvedScale.viewport"},{"type":"text","value":" that it owns.\nA logarithmic content domain and viewport domain must contain finite, nonzero\nnumbers and remain on the same side of zero."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The resolved scale exposes both coordinate systems:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { contentDomain, domain, translate, map } = scene.scales.x.viewport!","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.scales.x.map(value)"},{"type":"text","value":" is the committed, untranslated coordinate used to\nconstruct geometry. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewport.map(value)"},{"type":"text","value":" returns its presented coordinate."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"non-cartesian-coordinates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-cartesian coordinates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Polar and geographic marks resolve geometry from the same final\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.chart"},{"type":"text","value":" bounds without materializing Cartesian x/y channels:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { polar, radialArc } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polar"},{"type":"text","value":" copies entries from its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales"},{"type":"text","value":" registry, assigns responsive\nangular and radial ranges, and renders guide backgrounds, child marks, then\nguide foregrounds around one resolved center. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"},{"type":"text","value":" calls an\napplication-supplied D3 projection callback or fits a projection descriptor to\ndata, a sphere, or explicit geometry."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both paths emit the same keyed scene nodes and interaction points as ordinary\nmarks. SVG rendering, DOM reconciliation, focus, export, and adapters do not\nneed a coordinate-system branch. Their outer chart uses\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales: { x: null, y: null }"},{"type":"text","value":"; no Cartesian guides are created."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These capabilities stay behind separate package subpaths so their D3 geometry\ndoes not enter a Cartesian consumer. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/polar-and-radar.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Polar and Radar Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/maps-and-spatial.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Maps and Spatial Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"band-alignment","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Band alignment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A band scale returns the start of a band. TanStack Charts centers the resolved positional value:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"band start ├──────── bandwidth ────────┤\n                         ▲\n                  mapped chart value","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This gives bars, dots, text, ticks, and interaction points a shared categorical center."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bars use the full primary bandwidth minus their "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"barX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'category',\n  inset: 2,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The band scale's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paddingInner"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paddingOuter"},{"type":"text","value":" determine category spacing.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"},{"type":"text","value":" removes additional pixels from both bar edges after layout."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For side-by-side bars, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layout: group()"},{"type":"text","value":" subdivides the primary bandwidth. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/bars-and-rankings.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bars and Rankings"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scene-and-pointer-coordinates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene and pointer coordinates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene nodes and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint.x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint.y"},{"type":"text","value":" use absolute scene coordinates, including the margin offset."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Application overlays can align to the plot:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const scene = host.getScene()\nconst overlayStyle = {\n  left: `${scene.chart.x}px`,\n  top: `${scene.chart.y}px`,\n  width: `${scene.chart.width}px`,\n  height: `${scene.chart.height}px`,\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DOM pointer coordinates must first be converted into scene coordinates using\nthe rendered surface bounds. Chart-owned focus and the first-party brush, cursor,\nand zoom behaviors do this automatically against resolved scales. A custom\ngesture can use the resolved scale's optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invert"},{"type":"text","value":" operation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"clipping-and-overflow","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clipping and overflow"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clip: true"},{"type":"text","value":" clips marks to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.chart"},{"type":"text","value":". Guides and legends remain outside the clip:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  clip: true,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic margins only reserve space for chart-owned guides and legends. Application HTML overlays, external controls, and custom renderer chrome own their own layout."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"complete-horizontal-ranking","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete horizontal ranking"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"horizontal-ranking-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"horizontal-ranking","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { barX, defineChart, ruleX } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { citywages } from './data'\\n\\nconst rows = [...citywages]\\n  .sort((left, right) => right.POP_2015 - left.POP_2015)\\n  .slice(0, 8)\\n\\nconst compact = new Intl.NumberFormat(undefined, {\\n  notation: 'compact',\\n  maximumFractionDigits: 1,\\n})\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    ruleX([0], { stroke: '#94a3b8', strokeOpacity: 0.6 }),\\n    barX(rows, {\\n      x: 'POP_2015',\\n      y: 'Metro',\\n      fill: '#2563eb',\\n      inset: 2,\\n      radius: 3,\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      nice: true,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: {\\n        label: '2015 population',\\n        ticks: { format: (value) => compact.format(value) },\\n      },\\n    },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().paddingInner(0.12).paddingOuter(0.06),\\n    },\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export interface MetroPopulation {\\n  Metro: string\\n  POP_2015: number\\n}\\n\\nexport const citywages: readonly MetroPopulation[] = [\\n  { Metro: 'New York–Newark–Jersey City', POP_2015: 20_182_305 },\\n  { Metro: 'Los Angeles–Long Beach–Anaheim', POP_2015: 13_340_068 },\\n  { Metro: 'Chicago–Naperville–Elgin', POP_2015: 9_532_569 },\\n  { Metro: 'Dallas–Fort Worth–Arlington', POP_2015: 7_206_144 },\\n  { Metro: 'Houston–The Woodlands–Sugar Land', POP_2015: 6_656_947 },\\n  { Metro: 'Washington–Arlington–Alexandria', POP_2015: 6_097_684 },\\n  { Metro: 'Philadelphia–Camden–Wilmington', POP_2015: 6_069_875 },\\n  { Metro: 'Miami–Fort Lauderdale–West Palm Beach', POP_2015: 6_012_331 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { barX, defineChart, ruleX } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { citywages } from './data'\n\nconst rows = [...citywages]\n  .sort((left, right) => right.POP_2015 - left.POP_2015)\n  .slice(0, 8)\n\nconst compact = new Intl.NumberFormat(undefined, {\n  notation: 'compact',\n  maximumFractionDigits: 1,\n})\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    ruleX([0], { stroke: '#94a3b8', strokeOpacity: 0.6 }),\n    barX(rows, {\n      x: 'POP_2015',\n      y: 'Metro',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      inset: 2,\n      radius: 3,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: {\n        label: '2015 population',\n        ticks: { format: (value) => compact.format(value) },\n      },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().paddingInner(0.12).paddingOuter(0.06),\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=horizontal-ranking env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface MetroPopulation {\n  Metro: string\n  POP_2015: number\n}\n\nexport const citywages: readonly MetroPopulation[] = [\n  { Metro: 'New York–Newark–Jersey City', POP_2015: 20_182_305 },\n  { Metro: 'Los Angeles–Long Beach–Anaheim', POP_2015: 13_340_068 },\n  { Metro: 'Chicago–Naperville–Elgin', POP_2015: 9_532_569 },\n  { Metro: 'Dallas–Fort Worth–Arlington', POP_2015: 7_206_144 },\n  { Metro: 'Houston–The Woodlands–Sugar Land', POP_2015: 6_656_947 },\n  { Metro: 'Washington–Arlington–Alexandria', POP_2015: 6_097_684 },\n  { Metro: 'Philadelphia–Camden–Wilmington', POP_2015: 6_069_875 },\n  { Metro: 'Miami–Fort Lauderdale–West Palm Beach', POP_2015: 6_012_331 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=horizontal-ranking file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This chart needs only the lightweight linear and band scale entries."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For responsive layout recipes, see "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/responsive-charts.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":". For the exact shape of scenes and resolved bounds, see "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/runtime-and-scene.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime and Scene Reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"surface-margin-and-plot-rectangle","text":"Surface, margin, and plot rectangle","level":2},{"id":"container-responsiveness","text":"Container responsiveness","level":2},{"id":"automatic-margins","text":"Automatic margins","level":2},{"id":"text-measurement","text":"Text measurement","level":2},{"id":"axis-guide-options","text":"Axis guide options","level":2},{"id":"multiple-axes","text":"Multiple axes","level":2},{"id":"scale-ranges-and-coordinate-direction","text":"Scale ranges and coordinate direction","level":2},{"id":"continuous-viewports","text":"Continuous viewports","level":2},{"id":"non-cartesian-coordinates","text":"Non-cartesian coordinates","level":2},{"id":"band-alignment","text":"Band alignment","level":2},{"id":"scene-and-pointer-coordinates","text":"Scene and pointer coordinates","level":2},{"id":"clipping-and-overflow","text":"Clipping and overflow","level":2},{"id":"complete-horizontal-ranking","text":"Complete horizontal ranking","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Charts gives as much space as possible to the plot while keeping chart-owned guides inside the surface. The normal path is container-responsive and uses automatic margins.\n\n## Surface, margin, and plot rectangle\n\nEvery scene has three nested regions:\n\n```text\nsurface: scene.width × scene.height\n└─ automatic or explicit margins\n   └─ plot rectangle: scene.chart\n```\n\n`scene.chart` contains:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartBounds {\n  x: number\n  y: number\n  width: number\n  height: number\n}\n```\n\nMarks, grids, clipping, pointer focus, and copied scale ranges use this resolved plot rectangle.\n\n## Container responsiveness\n\nOmit `width` on the DOM host or framework adapter:\n\n```tsx\n<Chart\n  definition={chart}\n  height={360}\n  initialWidth={640}\n  ariaLabel=\"Monthly revenue\"\n/>\n```\n\nThe host observes its container and coalesces width changes into an animation frame. `initialWidth` is used when a real width is not yet available and for deterministic server output.\n\nUse `aspectRatio` when height should follow width:\n\n```tsx\n<Chart\n  definition={chart}\n  aspectRatio={16 / 9}\n  initialWidth={640}\n  ariaLabel=\"Monthly revenue\"\n/>\n```\n\nSet a fixed `width` only for an intentionally fixed graphic such as export, print, or email.\n\nResponsive definitions receive the current `width` and `height`, so presentation can adapt to the chart container:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart(({ width }) => ({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: xScale,\n      axis: {\n        ticks: { count: width < 420 ? 4 : 8 },\n        tickLabels: { rotate: width < 520 ? -30 : undefined },\n      },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: yScale,\n      axis: { label: width < 480 ? undefined : 'Weekly downloads' },\n    },\n  },\n}))\n```\n\n## Automatic margins\n\nLeave `margin` undefined for normal charts. The layout solver accounts for:\n\n- Formatted tick-label width and height\n- Rotated tick-label bounds\n- First and last tick overhang\n- Axis titles and their offsets\n- Text-mark bounds, including anchors, pixel offsets, and rotation\n- Current container font metrics\n- Optional color legends\n\nThe solver may resolve scales and text-mark positions more than once, but marks\nrender once against the final plot rectangle. `clip: true` keeps the plot\nboundary authoritative, so clipped text does not expand automatic margins.\n\nExplicit margins lock only the sides you provide:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  margin: { left: 80 },\n})\n```\n\nHere the left margin is exactly `80`; top, right, and bottom remain automatic.\n\n`margin: 0` locks every side to zero:\n\n```ts\nconst sparkline = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(values)],\n  guides: false,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  margin: 0,\n})\n```\n\nUse that combination for sparklines and intentionally chrome-free embedded graphics.\n\n## Text measurement\n\nStatic scenes use deterministic text estimates. The DOM host and browser\nframework adapters measure painted glyph bounds with the chart container's\ninherited font and relayout after web fonts load.\n\nAdvanced renderers can supply `measureText`. Its metrics include painted x and y offsets relative to the requested anchor and baseline, not only width and height. This is necessary for correct containment of rotated and anchored labels.\n\nAutomatic margins contain chart-owned guides and Cartesian `text` marks. Axis\ntick labels are thinned against their measured, optionally rotated bounds.\nExplicit text placement remains responsible for data-label collisions.\n\n## Axis guide options\n\nEach axis combines a required scale factory or instance with optional guide controls:\n\n```ts\nconst x = {\n  scale: xScale,\n  grid: false,\n  axis: {\n    ticks: {\n      count: 6,\n      format: (date: Date) => monthFormatter.format(date),\n    },\n    tickLabels: { rotate: -30 },\n    label: {\n      text: 'Month',\n      offset: 12,\n      fontSize: 13,\n      fontWeight: 600,\n      fill: '#334155',\n      opacity: 0.9,\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\n| Option            | Purpose                                                  |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `axis`            | Configure the axis or hide it with `false`               |\n| `axis.line`       | Show, hide, or style the baseline                        |\n| `axis.ticks`      | Configure candidates, stubs, padding, and formatting     |\n| `axis.tickLabels` | Configure label rotation and collision thinning          |\n| `axis.label`      | Configure axis title text, typography, paint, and offset |\n| `grid`            | Draw or style grid lines at semantic candidates          |\n| `reverse`         | Reverse the responsive range                             |\n\nBoth grids default to hidden when `grid` is omitted. A style object enables\nthe grid or baseline and accepts renderer-neutral stroke, opacity, width,\ndash, and line-cap fields.\n\nCandidate generation and label layout are separate. Choose at most one of\n`axis.ticks.count`, `axis.ticks.spacing`, and `axis.ticks.values`. Grid lines\nand tick stubs use the generated candidates; label thinning does not remove\neither. `axis.ticks.size: 0` removes stubs while retaining labels and grid\nlines.\n\nRotation and thinning are independent. Thinning is enabled by default and\nuses measured rotated bounds:\n\n```ts\nconst x = {\n  scale: xScale,\n  axis: {\n    ticks: { spacing: 80 },\n    tickLabels: {\n      rotate: -35,\n      thin: { minGap: 8, priority: 'ends', keep: importantDates },\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nHard-kept labels are retained even when they collide. Values absent from the\ncandidate set add labels only.\n\nHide one guide without removing its scale:\n\n```ts\nconst x = {\n  scale: xScale,\n  axis: false,\n}\n```\n\nHide every axis and grid while keeping scales for marks:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  guides: false,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nSet a reserved scale to `null` only when no mark uses that dimension. For\nexample, a `ruleY`-only chart uses `scales: { x: null, y: { scale: yScale } }`.\n\n## Multiple axes\n\nAdd a named scale when one coordinate system needs an independent mapping.\nDeclare whether it maps x or y, choose its axis side, then bind the relevant\nmark to its ID:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(revenue, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    lineY(margin, {\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'percent',\n      yScale: 'margin',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: dateScale },\n    y: { scale: revenueScale, axis: { label: 'Revenue' } },\n    margin: {\n      channel: 'y',\n      scale: marginScale,\n      side: 'right',\n      axis: { label: 'Margin' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`xScale` and `yScale` bind marks to scale IDs, not axis IDs. Axes visualize the\nscale registry entries. Multiple axes on one side stack outward and take part\nin automatic margin measurement.\n\n## Scale ranges and coordinate direction\n\nScale factories derive domains from marks. Configured instances retain fixed\nsemantic domains. TanStack Charts supplies ranges from `scene.chart`.\n\nFor a normal cartesian chart:\n\n- x increases from the left edge to the right edge.\n- continuous y increases from the bottom edge to the top edge.\n- a y band scale lays categories from top to bottom.\n\n`reverse: true` flips the range. It does not reorder or mutate the source domain.\n\nSee [Scales](./scales-and-d3.md) for scale selection, responsive ownership, and\npixel-to-value inversion.\n\n## Continuous viewports\n\nA continuous axis can present one semantic window of a larger content domain:\n\n```ts\nconst x = {\n  scale: scaleUtc().domain([historyStart, historyEnd]),\n  viewport: {\n    domain: [visibleStart, visibleEnd],\n    translate: dragOffset,\n  },\n}\n```\n\nThe configured or inferred scale domain describes the complete content.\n`viewport.domain` is the committed semantic window used for mapping, axes, and\ngrid lines. `viewport.translate` is a transient output-space offset applied\nafter that mapping.\n\nViewport ownership is resolved for each mark and each axis. A mark that\nmaterializes an active viewport axis is content on that axis by default. The\ncompiler gives each such mark its own plot-bounded clip layer and applies only\nthe translations for axes it owns. Axes and grid lines stay fixed. Marks that\ndo not depend on the translated axis also stay fixed, such as a frame or a\ny-only annotation during an x drag. Custom marks can override either axis as\n`'content'` or `'fixed'` through `InitializedMark.viewport`.\n\nMarks, focus layers, interaction points, and tooltip anchors use the same\npresented coordinates. `scene.points` retains every content point, including\noff-window points, for rendering and diagnostics. The interaction host limits\npointer strategies and keyboard navigation to clipped content points whose\npresented anchors are inside the plot clip. Points from marks with fixed\nviewport ownership remain candidates outside the plot.\n`viewportInteractionPoints(scene)` returns that subset without changing\n`scene.points`.\n\nTranslation is expressed in screen-direction scene pixels: positive x moves\ncontent right, negative x moves it left, positive y moves it down, and negative\ny moves it up. Domain order and `reverse` do not change those directions.\n\nThis makes paged history one chart and one continuous line rather than a guide\nchart overlaid with several plot charts. During a drag, keep the committed\ndomain fixed and update only `translate`. To settle one page, animate the\ntranslation to one plot width, then update the semantic domain and reset the\ntranslation to zero in the same application commit.\n\nViewport domains accept two distinct finite numbers or two distinct finite\nDates. The scale must be configured or inferable, continuous, invertible,\nunclamped, and independently accept domain and range assignment. Band,\nordinal, quantize, clamped, and getter-only scales are rejected. An authored\n`axis.viewport` cannot be applied to an opaque custom `ChartScale`; a custom\nresolver can instead return a complete `ResolvedScale.viewport` that it owns.\nA logarithmic content domain and viewport domain must contain finite, nonzero\nnumbers and remain on the same side of zero.\n\nThe resolved scale exposes both coordinate systems:\n\n```ts\nconst { contentDomain, domain, translate, map } = scene.scales.x.viewport!\n```\n\n`scene.scales.x.map(value)` is the committed, untranslated coordinate used to\nconstruct geometry. `viewport.map(value)` returns its presented coordinate.\n\n## Non-cartesian coordinates\n\nPolar and geographic marks resolve geometry from the same final\n`scene.chart` bounds without materializing Cartesian x/y channels:\n\n```ts\nimport { polar, radialArc } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'\n```\n\n`polar` copies entries from its own `scales` registry, assigns responsive\nangular and radial ranges, and renders guide backgrounds, child marks, then\nguide foregrounds around one resolved center. `geoShape` calls an\napplication-supplied D3 projection callback or fits a projection descriptor to\ndata, a sphere, or explicit geometry.\n\nBoth paths emit the same keyed scene nodes and interaction points as ordinary\nmarks. SVG rendering, DOM reconciliation, focus, export, and adapters do not\nneed a coordinate-system branch. Their outer chart uses\n`scales: { x: null, y: null }`; no Cartesian guides are created.\n\nThese capabilities stay behind separate package subpaths so their D3 geometry\ndoes not enter a Cartesian consumer. See\n[Polar and Radar Charts](../examples/polar-and-radar.md) and\n[Maps and Spatial Charts](../examples/maps-and-spatial.md).\n\n## Band alignment\n\nA band scale returns the start of a band. TanStack Charts centers the resolved positional value:\n\n```text\nband start ├──────── bandwidth ────────┤\n                         ▲\n                  mapped chart value\n```\n\nThis gives bars, dots, text, ticks, and interaction points a shared categorical center.\n\nBars use the full primary bandwidth minus their `inset`:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'category',\n  inset: 2,\n})\n```\n\nThe band scale's `paddingInner` and `paddingOuter` determine category spacing.\n`inset` removes additional pixels from both bar edges after layout.\n\nFor side-by-side bars, `layout: group()` subdivides the primary bandwidth. See\n[Bars and Rankings](../examples/bars-and-rankings.md).\n\n## Scene and pointer coordinates\n\nScene nodes and `ChartPoint.x` and `ChartPoint.y` use absolute scene coordinates, including the margin offset.\n\nApplication overlays can align to the plot:\n\n```ts\nconst scene = host.getScene()\nconst overlayStyle = {\n  left: `${scene.chart.x}px`,\n  top: `${scene.chart.y}px`,\n  width: `${scene.chart.width}px`,\n  height: `${scene.chart.height}px`,\n}\n```\n\nDOM pointer coordinates must first be converted into scene coordinates using\nthe rendered surface bounds. Chart-owned focus and the first-party brush, cursor,\nand zoom behaviors do this automatically against resolved scales. A custom\ngesture can use the resolved scale's optional `invert` operation.\n\n## Clipping and overflow\n\n`clip: true` clips marks to `scene.chart`. Guides and legends remain outside the clip:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  clip: true,\n})\n```\n\nAutomatic margins only reserve space for chart-owned guides and legends. Application HTML overlays, external controls, and custom renderer chrome own their own layout.\n\n## Complete horizontal ranking\n\n```ts group=horizontal-ranking env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { barX, defineChart, ruleX } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { citywages } from './data'\n\nconst rows = [...citywages]\n  .sort((left, right) => right.POP_2015 - left.POP_2015)\n  .slice(0, 8)\n\nconst compact = new Intl.NumberFormat(undefined, {\n  notation: 'compact',\n  maximumFractionDigits: 1,\n})\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    ruleX([0], { stroke: '#94a3b8', strokeOpacity: 0.6 }),\n    barX(rows, {\n      x: 'POP_2015',\n      y: 'Metro',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      inset: 2,\n      radius: 3,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: {\n        label: '2015 population',\n        ticks: { format: (value) => compact.format(value) },\n      },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().paddingInner(0.12).paddingOuter(0.06),\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=horizontal-ranking file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface MetroPopulation {\n  Metro: string\n  POP_2015: number\n}\n\nexport const citywages: readonly MetroPopulation[] = [\n  { Metro: 'New York–Newark–Jersey City', POP_2015: 20_182_305 },\n  { Metro: 'Los Angeles–Long Beach–Anaheim', POP_2015: 13_340_068 },\n  { Metro: 'Chicago–Naperville–Elgin', POP_2015: 9_532_569 },\n  { Metro: 'Dallas–Fort Worth–Arlington', POP_2015: 7_206_144 },\n  { Metro: 'Houston–The Woodlands–Sugar Land', POP_2015: 6_656_947 },\n  { Metro: 'Washington–Arlington–Alexandria', POP_2015: 6_097_684 },\n  { Metro: 'Philadelphia–Camden–Wilmington', POP_2015: 6_069_875 },\n  { Metro: 'Miami–Fort Lauderdale–West Palm Beach', POP_2015: 6_012_331 },\n]\n```\n\nThis chart needs only the lightweight linear and band scale entries.\n\nFor responsive layout recipes, see [Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md). For the exact shape of scenes and resolved bounds, see [Runtime and Scene Reference](../reference/runtime-and-scene.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Layout, Axes, and Coordinates\ndescription: Learn how responsive ranges, automatic margins, guides, band alignment, and scene coordinates work.\n---\n\nTanStack Charts gives as much space as possible to the plot while keeping chart-owned guides inside the surface. The normal path is container-responsive and uses automatic margins.\n\n## Surface, margin, and plot rectangle\n\nEvery scene has three nested regions:\n\n```text\nsurface: scene.width × scene.height\n└─ automatic or explicit margins\n   └─ plot rectangle: scene.chart\n```\n\n`scene.chart` contains:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartBounds {\n  x: number\n  y: number\n  width: number\n  height: number\n}\n```\n\nMarks, grids, clipping, pointer focus, and copied scale ranges use this resolved plot rectangle.\n\n## Container responsiveness\n\nOmit `width` on the DOM host or framework adapter:\n\n```tsx\n<Chart\n  definition={chart}\n  height={360}\n  initialWidth={640}\n  ariaLabel=\"Monthly revenue\"\n/>\n```\n\nThe host observes its container and coalesces width changes into an animation frame. `initialWidth` is used when a real width is not yet available and for deterministic server output.\n\nUse `aspectRatio` when height should follow width:\n\n```tsx\n<Chart\n  definition={chart}\n  aspectRatio={16 / 9}\n  initialWidth={640}\n  ariaLabel=\"Monthly revenue\"\n/>\n```\n\nSet a fixed `width` only for an intentionally fixed graphic such as export, print, or email.\n\nResponsive definitions receive the current `width` and `height`, so presentation can adapt to the chart container:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart(({ width }) => ({\n  marks: [lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'value' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: xScale,\n      axis: {\n        ticks: { count: width < 420 ? 4 : 8 },\n        tickLabels: { rotate: width < 520 ? -30 : undefined },\n      },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: yScale,\n      axis: { label: width < 480 ? undefined : 'Weekly downloads' },\n    },\n  },\n}))\n```\n\n## Automatic margins\n\nLeave `margin` undefined for normal charts. The layout solver accounts for:\n\n- Formatted tick-label width and height\n- Rotated tick-label bounds\n- First and last tick overhang\n- Axis titles and their offsets\n- Text-mark bounds, including anchors, pixel offsets, and rotation\n- Current container font metrics\n- Optional color legends\n\nThe solver may resolve scales and text-mark positions more than once, but marks\nrender once against the final plot rectangle. `clip: true` keeps the plot\nboundary authoritative, so clipped text does not expand automatic margins.\n\nExplicit margins lock only the sides you provide:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  margin: { left: 80 },\n})\n```\n\nHere the left margin is exactly `80`; top, right, and bottom remain automatic.\n\n`margin: 0` locks every side to zero:\n\n```ts\nconst sparkline = defineChart({\n  marks: [lineY(values)],\n  guides: false,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  margin: 0,\n})\n```\n\nUse that combination for sparklines and intentionally chrome-free embedded graphics.\n\n## Text measurement\n\nStatic scenes use deterministic text estimates. The DOM host and browser\nframework adapters measure painted glyph bounds with the chart container's\ninherited font and relayout after web fonts load.\n\nAdvanced renderers can supply `measureText`. Its metrics include painted x and y offsets relative to the requested anchor and baseline, not only width and height. This is necessary for correct containment of rotated and anchored labels.\n\nAutomatic margins contain chart-owned guides and Cartesian `text` marks. Axis\ntick labels are thinned against their measured, optionally rotated bounds.\nExplicit text placement remains responsible for data-label collisions.\n\n## Axis guide options\n\nEach axis combines a required scale factory or instance with optional guide controls:\n\n```ts\nconst x = {\n  scale: xScale,\n  grid: false,\n  axis: {\n    ticks: {\n      count: 6,\n      format: (date: Date) => monthFormatter.format(date),\n    },\n    tickLabels: { rotate: -30 },\n    label: {\n      text: 'Month',\n      offset: 12,\n      fontSize: 13,\n      fontWeight: 600,\n      fill: '#334155',\n      opacity: 0.9,\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\n| Option            | Purpose                                                  |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `axis`            | Configure the axis or hide it with `false`               |\n| `axis.line`       | Show, hide, or style the baseline                        |\n| `axis.ticks`      | Configure candidates, stubs, padding, and formatting     |\n| `axis.tickLabels` | Configure label rotation and collision thinning          |\n| `axis.label`      | Configure axis title text, typography, paint, and offset |\n| `grid`            | Draw or style grid lines at semantic candidates          |\n| `reverse`         | Reverse the responsive range                             |\n\nBoth grids default to hidden when `grid` is omitted. A style object enables\nthe grid or baseline and accepts renderer-neutral stroke, opacity, width,\ndash, and line-cap fields.\n\nCandidate generation and label layout are separate. Choose at most one of\n`axis.ticks.count`, `axis.ticks.spacing`, and `axis.ticks.values`. Grid lines\nand tick stubs use the generated candidates; label thinning does not remove\neither. `axis.ticks.size: 0` removes stubs while retaining labels and grid\nlines.\n\nRotation and thinning are independent. Thinning is enabled by default and\nuses measured rotated bounds:\n\n```ts\nconst x = {\n  scale: xScale,\n  axis: {\n    ticks: { spacing: 80 },\n    tickLabels: {\n      rotate: -35,\n      thin: { minGap: 8, priority: 'ends', keep: importantDates },\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nHard-kept labels are retained even when they collide. Values absent from the\ncandidate set add labels only.\n\nHide one guide without removing its scale:\n\n```ts\nconst x = {\n  scale: xScale,\n  axis: false,\n}\n```\n\nHide every axis and grid while keeping scales for marks:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  guides: false,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nSet a reserved scale to `null` only when no mark uses that dimension. For\nexample, a `ruleY`-only chart uses `scales: { x: null, y: { scale: yScale } }`.\n\n## Multiple axes\n\nAdd a named scale when one coordinate system needs an independent mapping.\nDeclare whether it maps x or y, choose its axis side, then bind the relevant\nmark to its ID:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    lineY(revenue, { x: 'date', y: 'value' }),\n    lineY(margin, {\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'percent',\n      yScale: 'margin',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: dateScale },\n    y: { scale: revenueScale, axis: { label: 'Revenue' } },\n    margin: {\n      channel: 'y',\n      scale: marginScale,\n      side: 'right',\n      axis: { label: 'Margin' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n`xScale` and `yScale` bind marks to scale IDs, not axis IDs. Axes visualize the\nscale registry entries. Multiple axes on one side stack outward and take part\nin automatic margin measurement.\n\n## Scale ranges and coordinate direction\n\nScale factories derive domains from marks. Configured instances retain fixed\nsemantic domains. TanStack Charts supplies ranges from `scene.chart`.\n\nFor a normal cartesian chart:\n\n- x increases from the left edge to the right edge.\n- continuous y increases from the bottom edge to the top edge.\n- a y band scale lays categories from top to bottom.\n\n`reverse: true` flips the range. It does not reorder or mutate the source domain.\n\nSee [Scales](./scales-and-d3.md) for scale selection, responsive ownership, and\npixel-to-value inversion.\n\n## Continuous viewports\n\nA continuous axis can present one semantic window of a larger content domain:\n\n```ts\nconst x = {\n  scale: scaleUtc().domain([historyStart, historyEnd]),\n  viewport: {\n    domain: [visibleStart, visibleEnd],\n    translate: dragOffset,\n  },\n}\n```\n\nThe configured or inferred scale domain describes the complete content.\n`viewport.domain` is the committed semantic window used for mapping, axes, and\ngrid lines. `viewport.translate` is a transient output-space offset applied\nafter that mapping.\n\nViewport ownership is resolved for each mark and each axis. A mark that\nmaterializes an active viewport axis is content on that axis by default. The\ncompiler gives each such mark its own plot-bounded clip layer and applies only\nthe translations for axes it owns. Axes and grid lines stay fixed. Marks that\ndo not depend on the translated axis also stay fixed, such as a frame or a\ny-only annotation during an x drag. Custom marks can override either axis as\n`'content'` or `'fixed'` through `InitializedMark.viewport`.\n\nMarks, focus layers, interaction points, and tooltip anchors use the same\npresented coordinates. `scene.points` retains every content point, including\noff-window points, for rendering and diagnostics. The interaction host limits\npointer strategies and keyboard navigation to clipped content points whose\npresented anchors are inside the plot clip. Points from marks with fixed\nviewport ownership remain candidates outside the plot.\n`viewportInteractionPoints(scene)` returns that subset without changing\n`scene.points`.\n\nTranslation is expressed in screen-direction scene pixels: positive x moves\ncontent right, negative x moves it left, positive y moves it down, and negative\ny moves it up. Domain order and `reverse` do not change those directions.\n\nThis makes paged history one chart and one continuous line rather than a guide\nchart overlaid with several plot charts. During a drag, keep the committed\ndomain fixed and update only `translate`. To settle one page, animate the\ntranslation to one plot width, then update the semantic domain and reset the\ntranslation to zero in the same application commit.\n\nViewport domains accept two distinct finite numbers or two distinct finite\nDates. The scale must be configured or inferable, continuous, invertible,\nunclamped, and independently accept domain and range assignment. Band,\nordinal, quantize, clamped, and getter-only scales are rejected. An authored\n`axis.viewport` cannot be applied to an opaque custom `ChartScale`; a custom\nresolver can instead return a complete `ResolvedScale.viewport` that it owns.\nA logarithmic content domain and viewport domain must contain finite, nonzero\nnumbers and remain on the same side of zero.\n\nThe resolved scale exposes both coordinate systems:\n\n```ts\nconst { contentDomain, domain, translate, map } = scene.scales.x.viewport!\n```\n\n`scene.scales.x.map(value)` is the committed, untranslated coordinate used to\nconstruct geometry. `viewport.map(value)` returns its presented coordinate.\n\n## Non-cartesian coordinates\n\nPolar and geographic marks resolve geometry from the same final\n`scene.chart` bounds without materializing Cartesian x/y channels:\n\n```ts\nimport { polar, radialArc } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'\n```\n\n`polar` copies entries from its own `scales` registry, assigns responsive\nangular and radial ranges, and renders guide backgrounds, child marks, then\nguide foregrounds around one resolved center. `geoShape` calls an\napplication-supplied D3 projection callback or fits a projection descriptor to\ndata, a sphere, or explicit geometry.\n\nBoth paths emit the same keyed scene nodes and interaction points as ordinary\nmarks. SVG rendering, DOM reconciliation, focus, export, and adapters do not\nneed a coordinate-system branch. Their outer chart uses\n`scales: { x: null, y: null }`; no Cartesian guides are created.\n\nThese capabilities stay behind separate package subpaths so their D3 geometry\ndoes not enter a Cartesian consumer. See\n[Polar and Radar Charts](../examples/polar-and-radar.md) and\n[Maps and Spatial Charts](../examples/maps-and-spatial.md).\n\n## Band alignment\n\nA band scale returns the start of a band. TanStack Charts centers the resolved positional value:\n\n```text\nband start ├──────── bandwidth ────────┤\n                         ▲\n                  mapped chart value\n```\n\nThis gives bars, dots, text, ticks, and interaction points a shared categorical center.\n\nBars use the full primary bandwidth minus their `inset`:\n\n```ts\nbarX(rows, {\n  x: 'value',\n  y: 'category',\n  inset: 2,\n})\n```\n\nThe band scale's `paddingInner` and `paddingOuter` determine category spacing.\n`inset` removes additional pixels from both bar edges after layout.\n\nFor side-by-side bars, `layout: group()` subdivides the primary bandwidth. See\n[Bars and Rankings](../examples/bars-and-rankings.md).\n\n## Scene and pointer coordinates\n\nScene nodes and `ChartPoint.x` and `ChartPoint.y` use absolute scene coordinates, including the margin offset.\n\nApplication overlays can align to the plot:\n\n```ts\nconst scene = host.getScene()\nconst overlayStyle = {\n  left: `${scene.chart.x}px`,\n  top: `${scene.chart.y}px`,\n  width: `${scene.chart.width}px`,\n  height: `${scene.chart.height}px`,\n}\n```\n\nDOM pointer coordinates must first be converted into scene coordinates using\nthe rendered surface bounds. Chart-owned focus and the first-party brush, cursor,\nand zoom behaviors do this automatically against resolved scales. A custom\ngesture can use the resolved scale's optional `invert` operation.\n\n## Clipping and overflow\n\n`clip: true` clips marks to `scene.chart`. Guides and legends remain outside the clip:\n\n```ts\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: xScale },\n    y: { scale: yScale },\n  },\n\n  clip: true,\n})\n```\n\nAutomatic margins only reserve space for chart-owned guides and legends. Application HTML overlays, external controls, and custom renderer chrome own their own layout.\n\n## Complete horizontal ranking\n\n```ts group=horizontal-ranking env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { barX, defineChart, ruleX } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { citywages } from './data'\n\nconst rows = [...citywages]\n  .sort((left, right) => right.POP_2015 - left.POP_2015)\n  .slice(0, 8)\n\nconst compact = new Intl.NumberFormat(undefined, {\n  notation: 'compact',\n  maximumFractionDigits: 1,\n})\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    ruleX([0], { stroke: '#94a3b8', strokeOpacity: 0.6 }),\n    barX(rows, {\n      x: 'POP_2015',\n      y: 'Metro',\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n      inset: 2,\n      radius: 3,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: {\n        label: '2015 population',\n        ticks: { format: (value) => compact.format(value) },\n      },\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().paddingInner(0.12).paddingOuter(0.06),\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=horizontal-ranking file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface MetroPopulation {\n  Metro: string\n  POP_2015: number\n}\n\nexport const citywages: readonly MetroPopulation[] = [\n  { Metro: 'New York–Newark–Jersey City', POP_2015: 20_182_305 },\n  { Metro: 'Los Angeles–Long Beach–Anaheim', POP_2015: 13_340_068 },\n  { Metro: 'Chicago–Naperville–Elgin', POP_2015: 9_532_569 },\n  { Metro: 'Dallas–Fort Worth–Arlington', POP_2015: 7_206_144 },\n  { Metro: 'Houston–The Woodlands–Sugar Land', POP_2015: 6_656_947 },\n  { Metro: 'Washington–Arlington–Alexandria', POP_2015: 6_097_684 },\n  { Metro: 'Philadelphia–Camden–Wilmington', POP_2015: 6_069_875 },\n  { Metro: 'Miami–Fort Lauderdale–West Palm Beach', POP_2015: 6_012_331 },\n]\n```\n\nThis chart needs only the lightweight linear and band scale entries.\n\nFor responsive layout recipes, see [Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md). For the exact shape of scenes and resolved bounds, see [Runtime and Scene Reference](../reference/runtime-and-scene.md).\n"}
